WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:04.990
The identity of Anakin Skywalker's father
has been a point of contention ever since

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:07.220
it was first brought up in The Phantom Menace.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:11.000
Now, however, we have a piece of information
that might shed some new light on the true

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.400
identity of Darth Vader's deadbeat dad.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:18.300
The major selling point of the prequel trilogy
of The Phantom Menace, Attack of the Clones,

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:22.619
and Revenge of the Sith was that we'd be able
to see Anakin Skywalker's origins, learning

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:27.390
more about who one of cinema's greatest villains
was before his fall from grace.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.160
In practice, well, we didn't get a whole lot
that we didn't already know.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:36.360
He used to be a Jedi, then he went bad, and
telling audiences that took six hours and

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.560
$345 million.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:43.000
There was, however, one Death Star-sized truth
bomb hiding in Episode I, and none of us were

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:43.960
really prepared for it.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:49.350
When Anakin's mom, Shmi Skywalker, is asked
about who his father is, she simply explains

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.380
that there wasn't one.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.820
Instead, she just woke up one morning to find
that she was pregnant.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.820
The religious overtones at play here are pretty
obvious: a virgin birth foretold in prophecy,

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.560
heralding the arrival of one who will bring
balance to the world.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:08.050
They're also a bit on the nose, even for Star
Wars a series where the guy who's really good

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:12.190
at piloting spaceships is named "Skywalker,"
the guy who doesn't want to join up with the

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:16.800
Rebel Alliance is named "Solo," and the first
hope for the Light Side of the Force to come

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.740
along in years is named "Rey."

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:23.940
These movies have never exactly been subtle,
but giving us a messianic figure via virgin

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.580
birth is on a whole different level.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.700
Except, of course, that there's a twist to
it.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.180
Rather than becoming the savior that the Jedi
have been waiting for, Anakin grows up to

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:38.060
put down a bunch of children with a lightsaber,
get rebuilt as a cyborg, and then commit full-on

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.660
genocide by blowing up entire planets.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:44.840
Not exactly what any parents would hope for
their child unless one of them happened to

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.240
be a tyrannical overlord devoted to death
and corruption, that is.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:49.880
But we'll get to that in a bit.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:54.479
Rather than letting its biblical overtones
stand on their own, Episode I did provide

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.200
a further explanation for Anakin's conception
although fans weren't exactly thrilled with

00:01:59.200 --> 00:01:59.700
it.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.560
Up until that point, the Force had always
been portrayed as being essentially, well…

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:04.420
magic!

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.180
It was a metaphysical presence that ran through
all living things and united the galaxy as

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:09.680
one.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:13.400
Then it turned out that it was actually just
a bunch of tiny bugs that lived in your blood.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:19.400
Episode I introduced the concept of midichlorians:
microscopic organisms found in the bloodstream

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:24.180
forming a symbiotic relationship that allows
their hosts to connect with and, well, use

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:24.760
the Force.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:29.360
The more midichlorians you have floating around
your circulatory system, the more raw power

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:31.580
you have when it comes to the Force.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:36.180
It's essentially a plot device, allowing for
a quick explanation of why Qui-Gon Jinn and

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:41.770
Obi-Wan Kenobi are so convinced that Anakin
is a big deal: his "midichlorian count" is

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:44.160
over 20,000, which is even more than Yoda's!

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:48.760
Unfortunately, the implication here is that
a bunch of tiny midichlorians got together

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:52.520
and decided that they should just go ahead
and make a baby inside some woman who had

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.640
zero knowledge that this was happening, let
alone why.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.260
That is, at the bare minimum, pretty weird.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:02.910
If nothing else, Luke Skywalker's grandfather
being a collection of sentient microbes that

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:06.489
construct babies as a hobby is going to make
things pretty weird when the whole family

00:03:06.489 --> 00:03:07.860
gets together on Life Day.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.260
"Awwwkwaaard."

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.120
Did you ever hear the tragedy of Darth Plagueis
the Wise?

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.400
We thought not.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.740
It's not a story the Jedi would tell you.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:22.060
It is, however, the subject of one of the
best scenes in Star Wars Episode III: Revenge

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:22.740
of the Sith.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:28.220
While watching a space opera, Chancellor Palpatine
soon to claim unlimited power as the Emperor

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:32.850
tells Anakin about a Sith Lord named Darth
Plagueis, whose mastery of the Dark Side of

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:37.319
the Force was so complete that he could not
only stop people from dying, but also create

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.420
life by manipulating midichlorians.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.440
"So that makes perfect sense."

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:47.820
As Palpatine tells both Anakin and the audience,
Plagueis passed down this "unnatural" knowledge

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.360
to his apprentice, who then killed him in
his sleep.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:54.760
This is the tale that leads Anakin, who not
too long ago wasn't able to save his mother

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:59.240
from death, to study the Dark Side and ultimately
become Palpatine's apprentice.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:03.879
What Palpatine doesn't tell us although it
is heavily implied in the original scene,

00:04:03.879 --> 00:04:09.150
given the subject matter is that Plagueis'
apprentice was in fact Darth Sidious, perhaps

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.600
better known as Emperor Palpatine himself.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.800
To find that out for sure, you have to dig
deep into the rebooted Star Wars canon and

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:17.580
keep up with the novels and comics.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:21.810
If you did, then you might already understand
that there was a deeper meaning to Sidious

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:26.500
telling Anakin about the knowledge of how
to manipulate midichlorians to create life.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:31.720
That all brings us to the release of Marvel's
Star Wars: Darth Vader #25.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:36.120
It's the final issue of the series the second
and almost certainly not the last Darth Vader

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:40.860
comic Marvel has published since Disney acquired
the property back in 2012 and the final chapter

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.300
of a storyline set on the planet Mustafar.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:47.340
If the name sounds familiar, it's not just
because it reminds you of the tragic patriarch

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:52.969
of a certain animated Disney flick it's because
this fiery world is where Anakin Skywalker

00:04:52.969 --> 00:04:55.960
"died," and where Darth Vader was truly born.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:00.560
It was the planet we saw at the end of Revenge
of the Sith, where Obi-Wan chopped off Anakin's

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:05.360
limbs and left him to die, and where Darth
Vader's ominous castle was seen in Rogue One.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:09.710
The comic fills in the gaps between those
two events, with Vader's return to Mustafar

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:13.680
and the construction of the castle alongside
a forgotten Sith Lord called Momin.

00:05:14.069 --> 00:05:18.920
In keeping with Vader's obsession with mastering
death, Momin himself has managed to persist

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:23.099
beyond the grave, existing as a spirit that
could possess anyone who donned the helmet

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:24.120
that he'd worn in life.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:30.080
To make a long story short, Star Wars: Darth
Vader #24 sees Momin betray Vader and use

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.600
the power of the castle basically a giant
magic key to resurrect his body.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:40.770
Yup, that's right: He literally uses the Force
to recreate life, just as Sidious told Anakin

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:42.200
all those years before.

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:46.680
Needless to say, since we know that Vader
continues to exist in the Star Wars universe

00:05:46.689 --> 00:05:52.249
and most moviegoers have never heard of Momin,
Vader defeats the traitorous spirit, and uses

00:05:52.249 --> 00:05:54.140
the portal for his own ends.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.659
Darth Vader #25 sees the Sith lord tapping
into the Dark Side in a way that we've never

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:04.260
really seen before: His soul leaves his body
and merges with the Dark Side itself.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:09.939
The visual is both striking and clear: this
is Vader as he is within the armor, a creature

00:06:09.939 --> 00:06:15.089
sustained entirely by the Dark Side, with
whatever good remains in him hidden far beneath

00:06:15.089 --> 00:06:16.700
the mass of anger and fear.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.479
As Vader journeys through the strange, hallucinatory
terrain beyond the portal, he's shown key

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:25.620
moments from his past, complete with disjointed
dialogue lifted from the films.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:30.840
Though the original spoken dialogue like that
instance in Episode I, where Anakin asks Padmé

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:36.660
if she was an angel sounded innocent in their
previous contexts, here they read straight-up

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:37.160
sinister.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.880
We see these moments as they happened in the
films, and then we get one that seems new

00:06:41.889 --> 00:06:46.529
a memory that Vader himself couldn't possibly
have, which must instead be something that

00:06:46.529 --> 00:06:48.760
he's learning from his communion with the
Dark Side.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:51.760
The image is of Shmi Skywalker, pregnant.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:56.560
At first, she's alone, accompanied by the
line she spoke to Qui-Gon back in Episode

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:58.700
I: quote, "There was no father."

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:03.780
Then, as Vader watches, the spectral image
of Darth Sidious appears behind her, accompanied

00:07:03.789 --> 00:07:09.689
by the word "unnatural" the exact word that
Sidious used to describe Darth Plagueis' life-creating

00:07:09.689 --> 00:07:11.260
techniques in Episode III.

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:15.760
The message here is far more clear than the
implication back in that scene at the space

00:07:15.779 --> 00:07:20.900
opera: Sidious himself had a hand in Anakin's
creation, and was manipulating his life to

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:26.879
bring down the Republic long before Qui-Gon,
Obi-Wan, and Padmé encountered him on Tatooine.

00:07:28.189 --> 00:07:32.389
Of course, like a lot of things in Star Wars,
not everything Vader sees during his surreal

00:07:32.389 --> 00:07:34.740
trip through the Dark Side is meant to be
taken literally.

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:39.460
Like Luke Skywalker's vision in the cave on
Dagobah, there's plenty of it that's meant

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:40.899
to be shrouded in metaphor.

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:45.279
There is, for instance, a scene where Vader's
soul has to fight its way past an army of

00:07:45.279 --> 00:07:49.559
Jedi who were killed during Palpatine's coup,
which is less about their ghosts actually

00:07:49.559 --> 00:07:54.110
showing up for the Jedi equivalent of revenge,
and more about Vader metaphorically "killing"

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:55.740
the person he was before his fall.

00:07:56.210 --> 00:08:00.620
The Kylo Ren quote that echoes backward through
time to accompany this moment, "Let the past

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:03.709
die; kill it if you have to," tells us as
much.

00:08:03.709 --> 00:08:08.009
There is another scene where this becomes
very important, though, and it's accompanied

00:08:08.009 --> 00:08:13.700
by what is unquestionably Vader's most famous
piece of dialogue: quote, "I am your father."

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:18.520
That in itself is a prophetic glimpse of the
future this story takes place long before

00:08:18.529 --> 00:08:23.499
Vader will utter those fateful words to Luke
Skywalker in Cloud City but it's also connected

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:24.300
to the past.

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:28.580
The phrase is positioned on the page so that
it's suspended between images of Palpatine

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:31.160
and Obi-Wan, Anakin's two father figures.

00:08:31.729 --> 00:08:36.020
Each of them guided Anakin as their apprentice,
and ultimately, each of them will fail him

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:36.800
in their own way.

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:41.630
In the vision, Palpatine and Obi-Wan face
off in the fight they never had in the movies,

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:42.950
with Palpatine winning.

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:46.800
Then, Vader strikes the Emperor down himself
to continue on his journey.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:51.260
The idea of these two men as father figures
plays into the Star Wars saga's prominent

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:55.800
themes, but that scene in particular echoes
what we see from Luke's vision on Dagobah.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:09:00.280
In that film, Luke strikes down the character
that we will eventually learn is his father,

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:03.520
only to discover his own face underneath Vader's
mask.

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:08.560
Here, in the comic, Vader kills Palpatine
with a shower of force lightning, which foreshadows

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:13.560
their final conflict in Return of the Jedi,
and recontextualizes what we see there.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:19.000
Just like in Empire, Vader's true face is
revealed, but he bears very little resemblance

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:19.660
to Luke.

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:25.000
Instead, his face, pale and ruined from years
of embracing the Dark Side, looks a lot more

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:26.220
like Palpatine's.

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:30.750
In the end, we're left with the question of
what all this means for the future of Star

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:31.240
Wars.

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:35.820
On one level, this is just another entry in
the saga's long line of sons dealing with

00:09:35.830 --> 00:09:40.380
the legacy that was passed down by their fathers,
matching right up with Luke and Vader or Kylo

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:41.540
Ren and Han Solo.

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:47.140
It's a recurring theme, and given that Palpatine
plunged to his death back in 1983, it doesn't

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:51.210
seem likely that it'll be anything more than
just another branch on the complicated tangle

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:53.280
that is the Skywalker family tree.

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:58.900
"I am your father's brother's nephew's cousin's
former roommate."

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:04.400
Or, at least, it wouldn't seem likely if we
were talking about this way back when.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:09.390
But since the release of the trailer for Episode
IX: The Rise of Skywalker, we've all heard

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:12.220
the familiar, sinister echo of Palpatine's
laugh.

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:16.980
Plus, we already know that Ian McDiarmid,
who played Palpatine in Return of the Jedi

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:20.280
and the prequel trilogy, is set to make an
appearance in the film.

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:21.920
Nobody saw that coming!

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:27.540
"We deal, as you know, in Star Wars — they're
very good at dealing in unexpected moments

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:28.040
the whole time."

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:32.520
Considering the timing of the issue and the
fact that its big reveal came after these

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:36.720
elements were already in place, it seems likely
that it's going to come up in some form when

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.320
Episode IX hits theaters.

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:42.270
If nothing else, we're going to be seeing
Darth Sidious knowing, for the first time,

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:45.500
that he's much more tied into the Skywalker
family than we thought.

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:50.340
Whether that's in the form of flashbacks,
a first Dark Side Force ghost, or even a shocking

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:53.940
return from a pretty definitive death, it's
bound to have an impact.

